HDR10+ dynamic metadata output (HDR10+ vendor InfoFrame), i915 and amdgpu

SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later

Ported from CroqueMr's CB1 (libreelec-x86-DV-pcon, upstream/main
9eb35aeb11, Linux 7.2.6), GPL-2.0-or-later:
- linux-9940-hdr10plus.patch: the connector property, the 31-byte HDR10+
  VSIF (OUI 0x90848B) validation, its packing/unpacking/logging in
  drivers/video/hdmi.c, the EDID HDR10+ VSVDB parse;
- linux-9941-hdr10plus-presentation-contract.patch: the packet belongs to
  the atomic commit that carries its framebuffer (primary-plane mask taken
  before helpers add planes);
- linux-9942-intel-hdr10plus-internal-recovery.patch (lessbones): i915's
  internal commits (link recovery) include the primary plane;
- the HDR10+ parts of CB1's i915 (linux-9900-00/01/04/07) and amdgpu
  (linux-9920) patches.
CB1 docs: CB1/docs/HDR10PLUS.md, docs/cb1/DRIVER-COVERAGE.md,
docs/cb1/HDR10PLUS-PACING.md. ST 2094-40 to HDMI field packing is done in
userspace (CB1 cb1_hdr10plus.c); the kernel takes the finished packet.

Changed for YBLOD's 7.3 and its Dolby Vision patches (linux-9900, 9928,
9930, 9931):
- VS DIP / vsp_infopacket ownership: the HDR10+ VSIF and the Dolby VSIF
  never share a commit. Connector atomic_check refuses HDR10PLUS_METADATA
  together with DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB=1 in the dolby-vision and lldv
  tunnel formats, and the i915 compute (intel_hdmi_compute_hdmi_infoframe)
  and HDR10+ check, and the amdgpu final atomic check, refuse it again.
  The hdr10 tunnel format sends no Dolby VSIF and may carry HDR10+: that
  is Dolby Vision to HDR10+ (the Vulkan engine's PQ picture through the
  tunnel, YBLOD 0.4.0). Its pipe is RGB 8 bpc carrying 12-bit YCbCr 4:2:2
  BT.2020 declared by linux-9928's own AVI InfoFrame, so for it the core
  check (drm_connector_check_hdr10plus(state, tunnel=true)) does not
  require max bpc >= 10 or a BT.2020 Colorspace, and the drivers' link
  depth checks are skipped. Readout: the VS DIP
  unpacks to the HDR10+ member of union hdmi_vendor_any_infoframe (whole
  union zeroed on compute), so the state checker compares like for like.
- Native HDMI connectors only: the property is attached to i915 HDMI
  connectors (display version >= 10, as CB1) and amdgpu HDMI-A
  connectors. The DP connectors of the 9930 (DP PCON) and 9931 (LSPCON
  level-shifter) Dolby Vision routes never carry it, and the checks also
  require DRM_MODE_CONNECTOR_HDMIA.
- Dropped with the output: on a connector without a CRTC, or whose CRTC
  goes inactive, the kernel drops the blob instead of failing the commit
  (drm_connector_hdr10plus_drop_inactive()), as YBLOD's i915 and amdgpu
  already do for DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB.
- i915: a new packet on an already-HDR10+ pipe is no fastset mismatch (it
  is written with the frame); the AVI CTA VIC replaces an HDMI 1.4b VIC
  (4K24/25/30) via drm_hdmi_vendor_infoframe_from_display_mode(), since
  7.3 has no drm_hdmi_avi_infoframe_vic(); CB1's GMP (gen9) and
  DVBRIDGE_HDR10_DEEP_COLOR depth changes are not taken: the link depth
  is i915's (and YBLOD's HDR 4:2:0 retry, linux-9923), checked >= 10 bpc.
- amdgpu (7.3 split amdgpu_dm_connector.c): the packet is set where
  YBLOD's DV stream setup is (dm_update_crtc_state()); a dc_stream_state
  hdr10plus flag makes HDR10+ entry/exit a timing change for DC (as
  dv_lab), so a stream's HDMI VSIF is never left as stale HDR10+ or the
  reverse; freesync's skip-modeset shortcut is not taken across it.
  CB1's amdgpu DV/3D/deep-colour work outside HDR10+ is not part of this.

Userspace contract (Kodi):

Property: "HDR10PLUS_METADATA", connector, blob, atomic. Present only on
native HDMI connectors (see above): its absence means no HDR10+ route.
0 = no HDR10+ (the normal HDMI VSIF is sent).

Blob: exactly 31 bytes, the complete HDR10+ vendor-specific InfoFrame as
sent on HDMI (HDR10+ System / CTA-861-H HDR10+ VSIF, the HDMI
representation of ST 2094-40 application 4, not the SEI payload):
  [0]  0x81 type (vendor-specific InfoFrame)
  [1]  0x01 version
  [2]  0x1b length (27)
  [3]  checksum: sum of all 31 bytes == 0 (mod 256)
  [4..6] 0x8b 0x84 0x90 (IEEE OUI 0x90848B, LSB first)
  [7]  bits 7-6 application_version (0 or 1, and <= the sink's VSVDB
       version), bits 5-1 targeted_system_display_maximum_luminance in
       32-nit steps ((nits + 16) / 32, max 31), bit 0 = 0
  [8]  average_maxrgb, 16-nit steps ((nits + 8) / 16, max 255)
  [9..17] the nine distribution_maxrgb percentiles for 1, 5, 10, 25, 50,
       75, 90, 95, 99 % (V0 with ten percentiles: the 99 % one, the 98 %
       one dropped), 16-nit steps; except in V1 byte [11] (the third
       slot), which is a percentage 0..100, or 255 = unavailable
       (101..254 rejected)
  [18] bits 7-4 num_bezier_curve_anchors (0..9), bits 3-0 knee_point_x
       bits 9-6 (knee point 10-bit: ST 2094-40 12-bit value / 4)
  [19] bits 7-2 knee_point_x bits 5-0, bits 1-0 knee_point_y bits 9-8
  [20] knee_point_y bits 7-0
  [21..29] bezier_curve_anchors[0..8], 8-bit (10-bit value / 4); the
       first num_bezier_curve_anchors must be non-decreasing, the rest 0
  [30] bit 7 graphics_overlay_flag, bit 6 no_delay = 1 (required: the
       packet applies to the picture it is committed with), bits 5-0 = 0
Single processing window only (num_windows 1); no colour saturation
mapping. The kernel checks the header, OUI, checksum, version, reserved
bits, no_delay, anchor count and order, unused anchor bytes and the V1
percentage byte when the property is set (-EINVAL), and sends the bytes
unchanged (i915 VS DIP, amdgpu vsp_infopacket). CB1's
cb1_hdr10plus_vsif() (CB1/src/cb1_hdr10plus.c, from FFmpeg's
AVDynamicHDRPlus) produces exactly this layout, with no_delay = true.

A commit with a non-zero HDR10PLUS_METADATA is refused (-EINVAL; use
TEST_ONLY to probe) unless all of:
- the connector is HDMI-A with an HDR10+ VSVDB in its EDID, version >=
  the packet's application_version;
- HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA is set: static metadata type 1, EOTF SMPTE ST 2084;
- Colorspace is BT2020_RGB or BT2020_YCC; max bpc >= 10 and the link
  actually runs at >= 10 bpc (i915 pipe_bpp >= 30, amdgpu stream depth);
  except in the DV tunnel's hdr10 format (12-bit 4:2:2 on an 8 bpc pipe);
- DVBRIDGE_DV_STANDARD_LAB is 0, or 1 with DVBRIDGE_TUNNEL_FORMAT hdr10;
- the same commit sets the CRTC's primary plane with a framebuffer (the
  picture the packet describes); CRTC active; no async flip (page-flip
  with DRM_MODE_PAGE_FLIP_ASYNC); no 3D mode;
- amdgpu: the stream is native HDMI TMDS with no HDMI VIC.

Lifetime:
- Per commit: the packet goes out with the framebuffer of the same commit
  (i915: written in that commit's vblank-evaded plane update, DSB/flip
  queue off while HDR10+ is on; amdgpu: in the same DC stream update as
  the surface). Each picture's commit sets a new blob; the kernel never
  repeats or advances packets on its own. A commit that does not touch
  the connector keeps the last packet (it stays on the link until
  replaced).
- Turning HDR10+ on (0 -> blob) or off (blob -> 0) is a modeset
  (ALLOW_MODESET): the VSIF changes owner and, for 4K24/25/30 HDMI-VIC
  timings, the AVI VIC changes. Do it with the HDR mode change at playback
  start/stop, not per seek: on seek keep the property set and send the
  new picture's packet with its frame.
- The property is a normal connector state value: it stays set across
  commits until userspace sets 0. The kernel drops it (reads back 0) when
  the connector loses its CRTC or the CRTC is set inactive; set it again
  with the next frame after re-enabling.
- Hotplug/EDID change: the VSVDB is re-read; a stale packet whose version
  the new sink doesn't support makes the next commit fail -EINVAL; clear
  the property and fall back to HDR10.
- i915 internal commits (link recovery) add the primary plane themselves
  (CB1 linux-9942), so they pass the frame rule with the last packet.

Untested on hardware: no HDR10+ TV in the YBLOD lab (LG sets have no
HDR10+). Compile-checked only (i915, xe, amdgpu, -Werror, on YBLOD's
Linux 7.3 series). Applies after linux-9931.

